WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.340
We were having a
story when last seen.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.420
I was back from the emergency
room the following day

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.600
and wondering, what
had happened to me?

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.110
And like all of you here,
hopefully all of you here,

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.260
I have the power of R and
RStudio, and I know

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:22.740
how to do an internet search.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.590
So I found a blog
post that told me

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.380
how to export all the data
from the Apple Health Export.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.080
And that's what I did.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.060
And I also know ggplot and
I did this chart.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.260
This is the very chart that
I took with me to my doctor

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.470
a couple of days later.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.860
It's not a very fancy chart.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.910
The vertical axis
shows my pulse.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.070
The horizontal axis
is the time of day.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.770
Zero is midnight.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:52.050
And you can see about

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.810
there is the event that sent
me to the emergency room.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.920
My perception of the event that
sent me to the emergency room.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.300
In the middle of the
night, about 1:30 AM.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.360
This is when I
was upset, got up,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.825
took some bicarbonate of soda.

00:01:04.825 --> 00:01:07.200
The whole thing did not last
very long, went back to bed,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.860
went to sleep.
While I was asleep,

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.030
my pulse was going up gradually,
which is very unusual,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.788
and then hit that
and off I went.

00:01:14.788 --> 00:01:16.830
And that's the story of
my visit to the emergency

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.680
room, which was not a big deal.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.130
It does provide an
example of how your health

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.150
tracker is basically making
a recording of your life

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.290
with data.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.220
So in order to do this chart and
to look into this a little bit

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.510
more, I had to dig into
the Apple Health Export.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.080
And the first thing that
sort of blew me away was just

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.840
the sheer volume of data.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.770
Never in my
professional life have I

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.120
worked with a data set
nearly as large as me,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.920
and I was kind of excited about
that and it was kind of fun.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.510
So 3.9 million rose, 1.2 million
rows in the last year alone.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:02.680
I mean, as we speak, I am
adding rows to the data.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:07.830
And I also did some additional
stuff to try to say. was

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.770
this night unusual?

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.500
I went back and tried
to look for other examples

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.600
and didn't find anything,
which was a good thing.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.220
So you have I have all this
incredible amount of data,

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.670
but if you think of it as
a recording of your life,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.980
but if you had an audio
recording or a visual recording

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.690
of your life, most
of those moments

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.050
wouldn't be very interesting.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.460
And the same is
true of the data.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.110
There's a lot of
data, which is dull.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.100
It's 70% of the data.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.190
Is this sort of low level
stuff, your heart rate,

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.020
your active energy as
you're moving around.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:40.740
And so on.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.900
But there are a bunch
of summary measures

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.680
and other categories
of measures.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.090
Basically, this is what
you see on the health app,

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.420
on the iPhone, or
you see these icons

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:58.890
with these categories of things
that it's keeping track of.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.600
And underneath each of
those or with each of

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.760
those are sort of
individual measures.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.682
And this is the
measure, the ones

00:03:06.682 --> 00:03:07.890
that would fit on the screen.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.450
There are quite a
few more than this.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:14.250
And so you can get into
any one of these things.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:15.860
Each of them has
a sort of a story

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.240
and maybe relevant
to different factors.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.850
So you have heart
rate, of course,

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:27.290
but you but you have
your stuff about sleep,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.020
which is not showing here
and step count and things

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.880
like that.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.935
So lets

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.010
talk about, what is the
Apple Health Export?

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.090
So basically on
the iPhone, there

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.350
is a database of all
of this information.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.020
And that's what's sitting
underneath the Health app.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:52.190
And Apple, if you're app
developer on the iPhone,

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.820
the API to that database
is called the HealthKit.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.022
And what's happening
is, is measurements

00:03:58.022 --> 00:03:59.480
are being fed in
from the watch are

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.930
being fed in from the phone.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:02.640
They're also coming
in from other apps.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.940
For example, I have
Lose It on my phone,

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.030
which is a calorie counting app,
which I have occasionally used.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.880
I have a blood pressure
cuff, which has a Bluetooth

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.347
connection to my phone.

00:04:12.347 --> 00:04:14.180
I also have it set up
so it has a connection

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.040
to my electronic health records.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.350
So it's all of the
records from my doctor.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.320
All of that goes
into this database

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.600
and it becomes accessible
via the export.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.320
And when you actually ask
for an export, in my case,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.750
it takes it about 9 minutes
before it can assemble

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:33.530
this whole thing and then I
can export it to my laptop

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.580
and load it into R.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:41.240
Now when I unzip this
export file on my laptop,

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.030
I think it's like 3 and
1/2 gigabytes, which

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.810
again is sort of crazy.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.370
It's a very wordy format.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.970
There's basically one
table that I care about

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:55.580
and that one is before I
process it in is like that one

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.690
is like 1 and 1/2 gigabytes.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.540
So it is a tidy table,

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.030
this table. You don't
have to tidy it up.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.560
It's already a tidy table.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.460
It's got one row per measurement
for my 3.9 measurements

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.920
or whatever with 3.9
million, whatever it was.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.570
And each row has a start,
time stamp, end time stamp,

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.490
the type of what
is being measured,

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:22.910
the value, and the source of
the measurement, the phone

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.740
or my blood pressure
cuff or whatever,

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.420
and the version of the software
that did that measurement.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.730
So you'll focus on
type and value usually,

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.220
but you need to pay attention
to the source and the version

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:35.820
as well, the source.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.990
So for example, your step count
is being produced by the watch,

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.430
but the phone is also
producing a step count.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:42.622
They both get fed in.

00:05:42.622 --> 00:05:44.330
If you don't pay
attention to the source,

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.660
you end up double counting.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.540
And I've had that happen
with other measures as well.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.070
So let's look at a measure.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:53.275
This is resting heart rate.

00:05:53.275 --> 00:05:55.400
I'm interested in, what's
the trend in resting heart

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.760
rate? We have on the
vertical axis, heart rate

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.180
on the horizontal axis.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.870
We have more than four
years worth of data here,

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.980
one point per day.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.040
And all of this feels
very personal to me.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.750
So I look at a chart like this.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.230
And I look at this thing and
I look at the smooth line

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.330
that I put through
this chart and I say,

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:15.900
whoa, what's going on here?

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.880
Why is this line doing this?

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.740
Is is there something about me?

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.020
Is, should I be
worried about this?

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.400
It's a trap you can fall
into by spending too much time

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.280
with your own data?

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.430
Should I worry about this?

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:30.690
Well, let's look at the version.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.160
So the vertical green bars
show the major versions

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.460
of the watchOS
software, and you see

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.260
that basically
what's happening is

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:40.850
between version 6
and version seven,

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.180
they changed the measurement.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.380
So what happened
with version seven?

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.430
They added sleep tracking.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.030
So before version seven, when
they were saying resting heart

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:51.890
rate, what they were
usually ending up

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:54.500
with was my heart rate
at 3:00 in the morning

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.810
or when I was asleep near the
lowest heart rate of the day.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.920
And they basically
redefined the measure,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.650
let's say improve the
measure so that it

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:07.010
was only while you were awake.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:09.493
You have to watch out for
this thing with any data set.

00:07:09.493 --> 00:07:11.160
You definitely have
to watch out for it.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.150
With the Apple Health
Expert, sometimes there

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.410
are clues to things like
this, but there are.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:17.760
Sometimes there are not.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.678
Sometimes it's a
Black box and you got

00:07:19.678 --> 00:07:20.970
to figure this out on your own.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.580
I'm going to show
one more example

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.420
because I like it a lot.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.510
This is a measure I'm very
interested in VO2 max.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:32.630
This is a measure of
your aerobic fitness.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.600
When it's done directly,
you're in a lab

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.660
and they're measuring
your exhaled gases

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.240
and seeing how well you're
using oxygen. Obviously,

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:42.750
you don't do that
with an iPhone.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:48.140
So they have an algorithm that
estimates your VO2 max based

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.170
on while you're walking
a certain distance,

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.210
you're looking at your
heart rate and the speed

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.010
that you're traveling
the distance.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:56.600
I look at this
chart, I'm actually

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.722
pretty good at inventing
explanations for things

00:07:59.722 --> 00:08:00.930
that don't have explanations.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:04.650
But I look at this chart
and I can't see anything.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.550
But if we add in
the version numbers,

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.470
we see that Apple is working
on improving their measures,

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:13.980
or I assume that's
what's going on.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:17.810
And so you see that between
version four or version five,

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:20.270
Let's see, version
five, six, it goes up,

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:21.630
then it goes back down.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.230
And then what's been
happening more recently

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:26.670
is less clear to me what's
going on with versions.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:30.090
Maybe stuff is, maybe
it isn't, I don't know.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:32.299
And you just have
to look at the data

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.809
and see. Now I would love
to know what my long-term

00:08:34.809 --> 00:08:37.850
trend is for this, to
watch this over time.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:41.559
But I look at this and certainly
for the earlier years now,

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:43.900
there's nothing I
could do with that.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.660
And so for all of
these measures,

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:50.640
you can dive in and get
into stories like this.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.820
So let's get into one
last measure, which

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:58.070
is different than
all the others, which

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:03.140
is the electrocardiogram, which
was added with the Apple Watch

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.130
four.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.170
This is different
because it actually

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:11.790
has FDA approval as a
actual medical device doing

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:13.650
the electrocardiogram
and then also

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:16.980
the software that evaluates
whether it's showing

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.520
signs of atrial fibrillation.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:23.130
Atrial fibrillation is a problem
with the electrical signal

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:25.500
in the upper chamber
of the heart.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.630
I should emphasize
for this population

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:33.000
that it's generally a problem
among the elderly, not

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.140
a problem for the
age demographic

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:38.010
that is typical of
this conference.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:39.900
You actually do the
electrocardiogram

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.840
by putting your finger
on the stem of the watch,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.330
as is shown in the picture here.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.930
And then to the right, we
have an actual little example

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.570
of the electrocardiogram
strip that it produces.

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:55.660
That strip is my strip,
indicates atrial fibrillation.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.150
That was from October of 2020.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.470
That's the second time I had an
episode of atrial fibrillation

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.260
in seven years.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.780
I am I am elderly.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.650
So it's not so far fetched.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.150
Got into the news recently
because of John Fetterman,

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.520
the Senate candidate
in Pennsylvania,

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.380
was in atrial fibrillation and
it contributed to his stroke.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:21.700
So what do I do in a
situation like this?

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.600
Well, I do a ggplot.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:26.610
So again, I went to
when I had my episode

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.920
of atrial fibrillation, I
went to the emergency room.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.960
My doctor is not
so surprised when

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.270
I walk in with a ggplot. I have
a beautiful blood pressure plot.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:39.600
So this in the case of
atrial fibrillation,

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.490
I literally used
the same function

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:45.860
that I had written several
years before for the first plot.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:49.230
In this case, I have
all the raw data

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.060
from the atrial
fibrillation episode, which

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:53.160
actually starts about 12:30.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:55.735
What happened during the day
was I was having a normal day.

00:10:55.735 --> 00:10:57.360
I went out for exercise
in the morning,

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.880
my heart rate slightly
elevated, all perfectly normal,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.350
sit down for lunch.
Part way through lunch,

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.950
I felt funny, check and
realize something is going on.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:09.150
So I have the raw
data for the electric,

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:12.408
for the atrial fibrillation,
but I don't have the expertise

00:11:12.408 --> 00:11:13.450
to do anything with that.

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.790
That's just beyond me.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:20.490
But what I was able to do was to
take the background heart rate

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:23.610
information and to put
the atrial fibrillation

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:26.080
episode in the context
of the rest of the day.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:27.780
And there is some
slight relevance

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:32.220
to when I was discussing
this with the doctor,

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.610
because what I could show was
that within a minute or two

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:39.750
of after the episode started,
there's an indication there

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.960
that shows I was doing the
electrocardiogram. So I could

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.170
tell, this is evidence
that I knew when I

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:46.420
went into atrial fibrillation.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:50.800
Sometimes people have don't know
they're in atrial fibrillation.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.560
But I knew.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.750
And you can also
see that I came out

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:58.680
of it before they hooked me
up to an electrocardiogram

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.540
in the emergency room.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.420
It actually goes on and off,
like throwing a light switch.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.910
So there was, none of
these things I'm showing

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:10.050
you have dramatic effects
on my medical treatment.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.355
In this particular case, there
was some slight advantage

00:12:13.355 --> 00:12:15.480
in having a discussion
about whether I needed to go

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.090
on blood thinners and whatever.

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:21.090
But the general point is,
is that this stuff shows

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:22.930
you a picture of your life.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.280
There's a lot of data there.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.530
If you have just looked
at Palmer penguins one too

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:31.770
many times and you want to try
something else with dplyr

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:32.530
you got.

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.100
You may have hundreds of
thousands and millions of rows

